  "final_text": "All four steps are done, using only the Kibana tools.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has the time field `timestamp`. Its fields include `Carrier` (string), `Cancelled` (boolean), `DestCountry`, `DistanceKilometers`, `FlightDelay`, `AvgTicketPrice`, and others.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-1506abc4 Flights' (id `s4-1506abc4-flights-7d8b8e411ce3`) has two panels:\n   - \"Number of flights over time\": a line chart of count over a date histogram on `timestamp`.\n   - \"Number of flights by carrier\": a bar chart of count split by the top 10 `Carrier` values.\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-1506abc4 Cancelled flights' (id `4f46bccf-92fd-43de-8748-4cc5bf25543c`) is an `.es-query` rule on `stackAlerts`.\n   - Schedule: every 1m.\n   - Query: `Cancelled` is true, over `timestamp`.\n   - Window: 5 minutes.\n   - Condition: count > 10.\n   - It was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n\n   I chose the query shape and the size of 100 myself, since the request didn't specify them.\n\n4. **Panel rename:** The carrier panel (index 1) is now titled 'Flights by carrier'. I changed it with `update_panel`, which rebuilt the panel with the same chart settings. Reading the dashboard back confirms the new title.\n\n**Read-back:** The alert rule reads back as enabled, with status `ok`.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
